      "strengths_deep_dive": [
        "IMANA's operational strength is rooted in its <cite id=\"3\">perfect 100/100 scores in Culture and Leadership</cite> from Charity Navigator. This institutional maturity allows them to manage complex <cite id=\"11\">foreign medical grants totaling over $2.5 million</cite> across multiple crisis zones. Their <cite id=\"5\">Candid Platinum status</cite> confirms that they track and share specific outcome metrics, such as establishing health clinics and hospitals for refugees. Furthermore, the organization\u2019s <cite id=\"12\">board of 12 members</cite> provides strong governance oversight for its diverse portfolio of medical relief and continuing education programs."
      ],
      "summary": "founded in 1967, the Islamic Medical Association of North America (IMANA) has spent over five decades providing <cite id=\"1\">direct patient care and health programs</cite> to underserved global communities. The organization operates as a professional network and humanitarian agency, managing <cite id=\"2\">$2,874,345 in total revenue in FY2024</cite> to support medical missions and disaster relief. IMANA holds a <cite id=\"3\">96/100 overall score from Charity Navigator</cite>, reflecting high standards of accountability and leadership. Their work spans across <cite id=\"4\">Gaza, Lebanon, Sudan, and Pakistan</cite>, where they deploy medical teams and establish clinics. Beyond international relief, IMANA maintains a <cite id=\"5\">Platinum Seal of Transparency from Candid</cite>, the highest level of disclosure available. The charity is <cite id=\"6\">verified as zakat-eligible</cite>, allowing Muslim donors to fulfill their religious obligations through medical aid. While the organization demonstrates <cite id=\"7\">strong financial health with 33.0 months of working capital</cite>, its program expense ratio of 68.7% sits below the 88.1% median for its peer group. This reflects a model that balances <cite id=\"8\">direct service delivery</cite> with a robust professional infrastructure for medical education and advocacy.",
